WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.361
- Making movies is eating a sandwich of.

00:00:04.361 --> 00:00:06.023
And the only thing that
gets a little better

00:00:06.023 --> 00:00:09.401
is as the years go by you
get a little more brave.

00:00:09.401 --> 00:00:10.827
But is always there.

00:00:10.827 --> 00:00:11.660
It was very well meaning.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:12.699
Why don't you take out the violence

00:00:12.699 --> 00:00:15.130
so you can reach a wider audience?

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:17.140
I said 'cause I don't
want a wider audience.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.253
I want the audience for the movie.

00:00:19.253 --> 00:00:22.757
I come from a country
where you didn't make money

00:00:22.757 --> 00:00:26.268
because you made movies, you
lost money as you made movies.

00:00:26.268 --> 00:00:28.656
And I was trembling and I was really moved

00:00:28.656 --> 00:00:31.732
because a, I was happy the
movie was being recognized

00:00:31.732 --> 00:00:34.959
and b, I was not going to jail.

00:00:34.959 --> 00:00:37.508
- He's a Mexican film
director, screenwriter,

00:00:37.508 --> 00:00:38.835
producer and novelist.

00:00:38.835 --> 00:00:41.218
He's had a lifelong
fascination with monsters

00:00:41.218 --> 00:00:43.841
which he considers to be
creatures of great power.

00:00:43.841 --> 00:00:46.297
His work can be characterized
as a strong connection

00:00:46.297 --> 00:00:48.375
between fairy tales and horror.

00:00:48.375 --> 00:00:50.757
He's Guillermo Del Toro
and here is my take

00:00:50.757 --> 00:00:52.900
on his top ten rules for success.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.577
Rule number one is my personal favorite

00:00:54.577 --> 00:00:56.169
and make sure to stick
around all the way to end

00:00:56.169 --> 00:00:57.659
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:57.659 --> 00:00:59.228
Also as he talking if he says something

00:00:59.228 --> 00:01:00.610
that really resonates with you,

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:02.026
please leave it in the comments below

00:01:02.026 --> 00:01:03.675
and put quotes around it so other people

00:01:03.675 --> 00:01:05.543
can be inspired as well.

00:01:05.543 --> 00:01:06.376
Enjoy.

00:01:09.304 --> 00:01:11.887
(upbeat music)

00:01:23.264 --> 00:01:27.074
- It started in the crib, I
was a baby and from the crib

00:01:27.074 --> 00:01:29.901
all the way to age 11 more or less,

00:01:29.901 --> 00:01:32.316
I had what is called lucid dreaming

00:01:32.316 --> 00:01:36.048
which means you dream that you are awake.

00:01:36.048 --> 00:01:36.881
- Hola?

00:01:37.881 --> 00:01:40.381
- So I literally saw monsters.

00:01:42.507 --> 00:01:45.706
I was used to monsters,
I love them but you know

00:01:45.706 --> 00:01:48.228
at the very early age
I made a deal with them

00:01:48.228 --> 00:01:50.468
to allow me to go to the bathroom

00:01:50.468 --> 00:01:55.343
because I was so afraid I
ended up peeing my crib.

00:01:55.343 --> 00:01:58.010
And you know, we stayed friends.

00:02:00.639 --> 00:02:03.077
You know there are only two
things you can do in art

00:02:03.077 --> 00:02:04.673
or in narrative in storytelling,

00:02:04.673 --> 00:02:07.709
you can tell about the good stuff in life

00:02:07.709 --> 00:02:10.204
which has always been very boring to me

00:02:10.204 --> 00:02:13.625
and you can tell stories
about the dark side of life

00:02:13.625 --> 00:02:16.508
which has been much more attractive to me.

00:02:16.508 --> 00:02:20.211
When I talk to pre-first time filmmakers

00:02:20.211 --> 00:02:23.586
or first time filmmakers,
I always tell them

00:02:23.586 --> 00:02:27.253
look, making movies is
eating a sandwich of.

00:02:28.677 --> 00:02:30.261
And the only thing that
gets a little better

00:02:30.261 --> 00:02:33.692
is as the years go by you
get a little more brave.

00:02:33.692 --> 00:02:36.460
But the is always there.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.365
- [Interviewer] But isn't
that true of life also?

00:02:38.365 --> 00:02:42.448
- I think it is, I use
another metaphor for life.

00:02:43.509 --> 00:02:46.619
But for film, I tell you that one fits.

00:02:46.619 --> 00:02:50.550
- [Interviewer] And in the
end somehow you find joy.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:54.717
- Well you end up convincing
yourself it's peanut butter.

00:02:57.829 --> 00:03:00.964
- [Interviewer] And is
it ever peanut butter?

00:03:00.964 --> 00:03:03.131
- I want to believe it is.

00:03:03.999 --> 00:03:08.740
- [Interviewer] But do you look
back and go yes I did that--

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:12.465
- I tell you without a
doubt, we may say this way,

00:03:12.465 --> 00:03:16.632
it's the greatest, being an
artist in whatever discipline,

00:03:17.619 --> 00:03:20.956
dance, painting, theater,
whatever you want.

00:03:20.956 --> 00:03:23.296
It's the greatest work on earth.

00:03:23.296 --> 00:03:27.220
I mean, it's truly disheartening
in this way or that way

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:28.659
but so is every job.

00:03:28.659 --> 00:03:30.667
So is every job.

00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:35.289
When I was younger I worked
with my father on real estate

00:03:35.289 --> 00:03:39.456
for five years, I was the worst
car salesman in the history

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:42.766
of car salesmanship--

00:03:42.766 --> 00:03:43.637
- [Interviewer] You were too honest?

00:03:43.637 --> 00:03:47.730
- Yeah, people would come
and say what about this?

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.592
I was , you know Glengarry Glen Ross,

00:03:49.592 --> 00:03:51.849
I would not get the knife set,

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:54.984
I would be the Shelley Levene.

00:03:54.984 --> 00:03:57.057
I would be out, out.

00:03:57.057 --> 00:04:01.140
It was terrible, my father
said you are terrible.

00:04:02.604 --> 00:04:06.698
The other vendors whenever I
went out the other salesman,

00:04:06.698 --> 00:04:08.674
whenever I went out to
client, they actually

00:04:08.674 --> 00:04:12.346
laughed behind my back, actually
physically were laughing

00:04:12.346 --> 00:04:15.539
behind my back and I was the worst.

00:04:15.539 --> 00:04:16.586
But in real estate I was good.

00:04:16.586 --> 00:04:17.866
I was good on real estate.

00:04:17.866 --> 00:04:20.345
And my father used to
say, you have to do this

00:04:20.345 --> 00:04:22.913
to show that you can
have an honest living,

00:04:22.913 --> 00:04:25.775
do an honest living, and what
I did is work on real estate

00:04:25.775 --> 00:04:28.427
in the mornings and I did
monsters in the afternoon.

00:04:28.427 --> 00:04:31.220
But I know that when you have a real job,

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:35.276
I painted highways one
summer in Wichita, Kansas,

00:04:35.276 --> 00:04:39.532
I mean a real job has equal
heartbreak and stuff like that

00:04:39.532 --> 00:04:41.115
and all the things.

00:04:42.522 --> 00:04:43.355
- [Interviewer] I think
it's one of the things

00:04:43.355 --> 00:04:45.734
people really misunderstand is as glorious

00:04:45.734 --> 00:04:48.068
it is to do the things
you're passionate about

00:04:48.068 --> 00:04:50.689
and to be an artist, it's still a job.

00:04:50.689 --> 00:04:51.723
- Oh it's still a job.

00:04:51.723 --> 00:04:53.057
- [Interviewer] It's still work.

00:04:53.057 --> 00:04:57.640
- Yeah, yeah, it is but God
bless anyone that can do this

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.826
is beautiful and you have
to truly be grateful.

00:05:00.826 --> 00:05:03.547
And the only way to
remain objective about how

00:05:03.547 --> 00:05:07.464
grateful you need to be
is present your present

00:05:08.353 --> 00:05:12.199
to your past self when
you're 20 years old.

00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:14.480
And if you go and talk to
yourself at 20 years old

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:18.596
and said would you like
to be doing this movie,

00:05:18.596 --> 00:05:21.352
with this budget, what do
you think your response

00:05:21.352 --> 00:05:26.166
at this training would be,
absolutely yes, you know?

00:05:26.166 --> 00:05:29.219
I think that financial
success is not success.

00:05:29.219 --> 00:05:32.899
You know to me when
people say, for example,

00:05:32.899 --> 00:05:35.475
I showed Pan's Labyrinth
and somebody said,

00:05:35.475 --> 00:05:37.542
why don't you take out the violence

00:05:37.542 --> 00:05:39.278
and it was very well
meaning, why don't you take

00:05:39.278 --> 00:05:42.344
out the violence so you
can reach a wider audience?

00:05:42.344 --> 00:05:44.478
Because I don't want a wider audience,

00:05:44.478 --> 00:05:46.784
I want the audience for the movie.

00:05:46.784 --> 00:05:49.972
Each movie has an audience that's it.

00:05:49.972 --> 00:05:52.054
It's not going to get more or less.

00:05:52.054 --> 00:05:56.466
If you with it, if you
amputate it, if you cripple it.

00:05:56.466 --> 00:05:57.687
That's all do.

00:05:57.687 --> 00:05:59.455
You know people dream of a bigger audience

00:05:59.455 --> 00:06:01.955
and I go, but is it the movie?

00:06:03.119 --> 00:06:06.248
If in order to make more people happy

00:06:06.248 --> 00:06:11.089
the Cinderella doesn't
get the prince at the end,

00:06:11.089 --> 00:06:13.875
I don't know, it's not the same tale.

00:06:13.875 --> 00:06:18.718
As much as I like the Disney
films of the renaissance

00:06:18.718 --> 00:06:22.248
of animation, I can't, every
time I see the Little Mermaid

00:06:22.248 --> 00:06:25.328
I think but she dies in the book.

00:06:25.328 --> 00:06:28.770
She dies at the end, that's
what made it beautiful.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.704
That prince was ultimately so stupid

00:06:30.704 --> 00:06:33.103
and I understand they are
reaching a wider audience

00:06:33.103 --> 00:06:36.197
but is it the tale Hans
Christian Andersen wrote,

00:06:36.197 --> 00:06:40.899
no way, it's a different
movie, it's a different tale.

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:45.366
So if you're asking me was
Little Mermaid successful

00:06:45.366 --> 00:06:49.283
as a film, I say it was
but it wasn't the tale.

00:06:53.478 --> 00:06:55.864
It's a great, I love those
films, I really think

00:06:55.864 --> 00:06:59.063
they're beautifully constructed
but it's not the tale.

00:06:59.063 --> 00:07:02.643
So Pan's Labyrnith without the violence

00:07:02.643 --> 00:07:04.427
is not Pan's Labyrinth.

00:07:04.427 --> 00:07:08.536
It is I don't know, it's a
fantasy like Harry Potter

00:07:08.536 --> 00:07:11.904
or Naria or any other fantasy film.

00:07:11.904 --> 00:07:16.408
And you know when they say,
I would like you to do this

00:07:16.408 --> 00:07:21.348
for me and I produce a lot
of first time filmmakers,

00:07:21.348 --> 00:07:23.432
but I don't produce all
first time filmmakers

00:07:23.432 --> 00:07:26.850
that approach me, I say look if I say no

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.393
and you give up, I'm sorry to tell you,

00:07:29.393 --> 00:07:31.751
it's the wrong job for you.

00:07:31.751 --> 00:07:35.657
'Cause you live with rejection for decades

00:07:35.657 --> 00:07:38.189
sometimes as a director
and you end up making

00:07:38.189 --> 00:07:39.820
the movie you want to make.

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:43.558
So if I say no, that
doesn't mean I'm right

00:07:43.558 --> 00:07:47.906
or I'm wrong, you just say
him I'll show him later,

00:07:47.906 --> 00:07:50.518
I'm going to make it and that fat bastard

00:07:50.518 --> 00:07:55.156
is going to have say, I was
so wrong and hit himself

00:07:55.156 --> 00:07:57.633
in the head because he didn't do it

00:07:57.633 --> 00:08:00.451
and I think that's the thing to do.

00:08:00.451 --> 00:08:04.120
Show us, don't tell us, do the thing.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:07.887
And if you do them wrong,
what you do on your on terms,

00:08:07.887 --> 00:08:11.451
that's how I define success,
failing in your own terms.

00:08:11.451 --> 00:08:14.928
I come from a country
where you didn't make money

00:08:14.928 --> 00:08:18.377
because you made movies, you
lost money as you made movies.

00:08:18.377 --> 00:08:21.985
And all of my life, when
I approach a new product

00:08:21.985 --> 00:08:26.556
and I'm 50 years old and
I don't know how many

00:08:26.556 --> 00:08:31.148
has producer, director, and
I still have the same emotion

00:08:31.148 --> 00:08:34.445
when I go into a project,
I go how much am I willing

00:08:34.445 --> 00:08:36.528
to lose to do that image.

00:08:37.533 --> 00:08:40.459
And there's always one or
two images in every project

00:08:40.459 --> 00:08:44.672
that you would mortgage
your house, sell your car,

00:08:44.672 --> 00:08:46.908
give everything you have to make sure

00:08:46.908 --> 00:08:49.075
that image comes to safe--

00:08:51.111 --> 00:08:52.607
- Yeah I've heard you
talk about Pacific Rim,

00:08:52.607 --> 00:08:54.094
it was the girl with the red shoes.

00:08:54.094 --> 00:08:54.927
- Absolutely.

00:08:54.927 --> 00:08:56.812
- You like had to make that
movie just to see that.

00:08:56.812 --> 00:09:00.145
- It doesn't matter what you go through.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:05.512
Doesn't matter if they
give you x amount of time,

00:09:05.512 --> 00:09:09.679
you need to always protect
the images that are crucial.

00:09:17.611 --> 00:09:21.111
(man speaking in Spanish)

00:09:23.763 --> 00:09:25.773
- [Interviewer] What did
Cronos achieve for you?

00:09:25.773 --> 00:09:29.145
- Well that movie changed
my life because we were

00:09:29.145 --> 00:09:31.305
more than the underdog,
more than the dark horse,

00:09:31.305 --> 00:09:34.161
we were a movie that
nobody wanted to produce,

00:09:34.161 --> 00:09:38.178
nobody was supporting except
my producers in Mexico.

00:09:38.178 --> 00:09:41.609
And we suddenly were selected for Cannes

00:09:41.609 --> 00:09:44.429
and literally we were all of a sudden

00:09:44.429 --> 00:09:48.596
one of the most awarded movies
in Mexican film history.

00:09:49.788 --> 00:09:53.955
I remember the first time
Cronos won, the first award,

00:09:54.877 --> 00:09:56.835
which was a cash award.

00:09:56.835 --> 00:09:57.668
- Oh.

00:09:57.668 --> 00:10:01.973
- And before that award we were
in debt for half a million.

00:10:01.973 --> 00:10:06.493
We won the award and literally
like a beauty contest,

00:10:06.493 --> 00:10:10.660
I was crying, crying on the
stage holding this giant check.

00:10:12.125 --> 00:10:15.179
And I was trembling and I was really moved

00:10:15.179 --> 00:10:18.069
because a, I was happy the
movie was being recognized

00:10:18.069 --> 00:10:20.943
and b, I was not going to jail.

00:10:20.943 --> 00:10:23.774
I used to do makeup effects for my stuff.

00:10:23.774 --> 00:10:26.934
They were very bad but I
was the only one doing them.

00:10:26.934 --> 00:10:29.986
So a lot of friends when
I was in my short films

00:10:29.986 --> 00:10:31.212
they would see the effect and say,

00:10:31.212 --> 00:10:33.551
who did that for you and I say, I did it.

00:10:33.551 --> 00:10:36.789
And they started telling
me, would you do mine

00:10:36.789 --> 00:10:38.356
and I started doing it and I realized

00:10:38.356 --> 00:10:42.523
there was an edge with which
going to feature films.

00:10:43.948 --> 00:10:46.573
And what I did was for
example, we did a TV series

00:10:46.573 --> 00:10:49.863
called Hora Marcada which
a terrible filmmaking

00:10:49.863 --> 00:10:53.230
but we were all learning,
Alfonso Cuaron was there,

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:56.170
I was there, Emmanuel Lubezki the DP,

00:10:56.170 --> 00:11:00.167
Guillermo Narvarro, my DP, all
of us started in that series

00:11:00.167 --> 00:11:01.771
and we were doing little experiments

00:11:01.771 --> 00:11:05.786
and I said to the producers,
I'll do the makeup effects

00:11:05.786 --> 00:11:08.796
for free, you pay the
materials I do them for free

00:11:08.796 --> 00:11:13.208
but in exchange, I write
and direct episodes

00:11:13.208 --> 00:11:15.290
and they said that's a good deal.

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:18.014
So it was a way to put
your foot in the door.

00:11:18.014 --> 00:11:20.661
And I was already on the way to do Cronos.

00:11:20.661 --> 00:11:23.661
I started writing Cronos around 1985

00:11:25.868 --> 00:11:29.833
and I knew that in order
to make the device,

00:11:29.833 --> 00:11:31.458
the Cronos device and the makeup effects,

00:11:31.458 --> 00:11:34.823
I needed to create a
company that would tell

00:11:34.823 --> 00:11:38.102
the producers there's someone
that can do those effects.

00:11:38.102 --> 00:11:41.721
So soon as I leave Cronos
essentially a year later,

00:11:41.721 --> 00:11:45.721
the shop was closed because
it had served it's purpose.

00:11:45.721 --> 00:11:47.631
- What's some stuff that
aspiring filmmakers can do

00:11:47.631 --> 00:11:50.521
to separate themselves from
the herd, from the noise.

00:11:50.521 --> 00:11:52.287
- Well I think be themselves.

00:11:52.287 --> 00:11:56.054
I mean, you can be hard to
peg, some people may like you,

00:11:56.054 --> 00:11:59.496
some people may not like you,
some people get what you do,

00:11:59.496 --> 00:12:00.992
some others think you're crazy

00:12:00.992 --> 00:12:02.985
but if you're true to yourself

00:12:02.985 --> 00:12:06.264
if you only do things
that you really believe in

00:12:06.264 --> 00:12:10.085
and that are personal
to you, then you know

00:12:10.085 --> 00:12:12.598
you don't need the
approval of anybody else.

00:12:12.598 --> 00:12:13.431
- Right.

00:12:13.431 --> 00:12:16.511
- That's in my opinion what distinguishes,

00:12:16.511 --> 00:12:18.374
not only the great horror filmmakers

00:12:18.374 --> 00:12:21.374
but the great filmmakers in general.

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:27.256
Some people can go through
life shooting B movies

00:12:27.256 --> 00:12:29.583
and eventually they get discovered

00:12:29.583 --> 00:12:32.055
you know by an audience that
caught them at the right time.

00:12:32.055 --> 00:12:36.051
Some people go all their lives
shooting sort of underground

00:12:36.051 --> 00:12:38.944
little things and they
can find their audience.

00:12:38.944 --> 00:12:41.095
And it's a matter of being true.

00:12:41.095 --> 00:12:44.648
Not trying to be somebody
else or have some different

00:12:44.648 --> 00:12:47.167
type of budget, I mean
you'll find your audience,

00:12:47.167 --> 00:12:49.129
and your audience will find you.

00:12:49.129 --> 00:12:53.667
I think what I strive
to is to connect deeply

00:12:53.667 --> 00:12:56.559
with audiences that like what I do

00:12:56.559 --> 00:13:01.128
even if the audience at
large does or doesn't,

00:13:01.128 --> 00:13:03.199
it makes no difference to me.

00:13:03.199 --> 00:13:06.991
I make weird movies no matter
what size the movie is.

00:13:06.991 --> 00:13:08.399
And they're not for everyone.

00:13:08.399 --> 00:13:11.095
And a lot of people may be puzzled or say,

00:13:11.095 --> 00:13:13.255
why did you do this but I only do things

00:13:13.255 --> 00:13:17.422
that I hope speak to somebody
in the same (mumbles) way

00:13:18.944 --> 00:13:23.192
was spoken to by people that
got high on their own supply.

00:13:23.192 --> 00:13:24.934
'cause I get high on my own supply.

00:13:24.934 --> 00:13:27.568
I make the movies because I want them.

00:13:27.568 --> 00:13:31.784
And whether it is robbers
and monsters duking it out

00:13:31.784 --> 00:13:35.201
or it's a post-war fable, whatever it is,

00:13:38.586 --> 00:13:41.181
they're done because I want to see them.

00:13:41.181 --> 00:13:45.396
Happiness is a very
difficult thing to define.

00:13:45.396 --> 00:13:47.698
I think it's fleeting,
you know, and I think

00:13:47.698 --> 00:13:51.662
that it's, I'm the
happiest when I'm working,

00:13:51.662 --> 00:13:56.562
and you know a project of
mine but even then, you know,

00:13:56.562 --> 00:13:58.031
I think it's a rhythm.

00:13:58.031 --> 00:14:00.498
I think you get into a rhythm of work

00:14:00.498 --> 00:14:05.055
and personal life that
you feel balanced about.

00:14:05.055 --> 00:14:08.799
But I think happiness is
sort of too chirpy a term

00:14:08.799 --> 00:14:12.357
to try to achieve, that
sounds like perfection.

00:14:12.357 --> 00:14:16.524
Both seem to me, equally
unattainable at its purest

00:14:17.373 --> 00:14:20.548
you know, I think you find peace.

00:14:20.548 --> 00:14:24.948
I think that's what I would
look for than happiness.

00:14:24.948 --> 00:14:26.916
- [Interviewer] Where on
a set do you find moments

00:14:26.916 --> 00:14:28.710
of great pleasure in the process?

00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:31.670
- Oh yes, discovering it is happiness.

00:14:31.670 --> 00:14:34.486
Whenever you see something that you wrote

00:14:34.486 --> 00:14:38.403
and re-wrote for six,
seven years come to life,

00:14:39.246 --> 00:14:42.431
it's just beautiful and
you're completely content

00:14:42.431 --> 00:14:45.022
and you feel fulfilled and it's magical.

00:14:45.022 --> 00:14:47.783
And it sounds like a cliche but it's true.

00:14:47.783 --> 00:14:51.950
Or when you see an actor in
wardrobe, make up, everything

00:14:53.048 --> 00:14:55.824
completely come into the
set for the first time

00:14:55.824 --> 00:14:57.498
is purely magical.

00:14:57.498 --> 00:15:00.916
I mean, there are many
things that are like that.

00:15:00.916 --> 00:15:04.593
In the course of discovering
or when you are with

00:15:04.593 --> 00:15:06.867
an actor and something doesn't work.

00:15:06.867 --> 00:15:08.891
Or with a camera and
something doesn't work

00:15:08.891 --> 00:15:12.045
and you find the solution and it's better

00:15:12.045 --> 00:15:14.299
than what you wanted to do first.

00:15:14.299 --> 00:15:16.742
Those are moments of great happiness.

00:15:16.742 --> 00:15:21.524
I've never thought of my
work or my life as a career.

00:15:21.524 --> 00:15:23.749
That's why I have doubts so many times.

00:15:23.749 --> 00:15:25.771
I want the right to up.

00:15:25.771 --> 00:15:28.579
That's the only inalienable
right of a human beng

00:15:28.579 --> 00:15:31.662
is to up and I cherish the screw ups.

00:15:32.644 --> 00:15:34.792
So you know, I'm going to continue up

00:15:34.792 --> 00:15:37.639
in the same way I've been
up since the beginning

00:15:37.639 --> 00:15:41.576
and the only problem is
of quality I can give you.

00:15:41.576 --> 00:15:44.363
You're going to get the
same from the same guy

00:15:44.363 --> 00:15:48.027
with the same earnestness
and I'm never going to worry

00:15:48.027 --> 00:15:52.255
about salary or career
or like that, I can't.

00:15:52.255 --> 00:15:57.027
I'm genetically engineered
to do my own thing you know.

00:15:57.027 --> 00:16:01.014
And that's what, if I
had been more malleable

00:16:01.014 --> 00:16:04.591
I have never given my version
of what happened on Mimic

00:16:04.591 --> 00:16:08.775
but I fought and won the
war, lost a lot of battles,

00:16:08.775 --> 00:16:11.331
but won the war and it
was a very tough war

00:16:11.331 --> 00:16:15.392
and it was a very
frontal war and I learned

00:16:15.392 --> 00:16:18.593
the value of identity in that process.

00:16:18.593 --> 00:16:22.447
And I tell you that's the
only thing I'm concerned with.

00:16:22.447 --> 00:16:24.967
I don't respond to the world.

00:16:24.967 --> 00:16:28.519
They say we're going to pay
you 10 million to direct

00:16:28.519 --> 00:16:31.352
an action, a straight action film.

00:16:34.403 --> 00:16:36.722
Can you imagine that,
waking up every morning

00:16:36.722 --> 00:16:40.889
and going to shoot a movie,
that's like, you know,

00:16:42.226 --> 00:16:45.290
not having a boner and going
to a sex show every day

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:47.373
for work, what do you do?

00:16:49.052 --> 00:16:51.849
Are you going to show up and
you're going to (mumbles).

00:16:51.849 --> 00:16:53.900
That's the same thing.

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:56.361
- [Evan] Pan's Labyrinth
is a project Del Toro

00:16:56.361 --> 00:16:58.968
had no problem showing up for.

00:16:58.968 --> 00:17:02.051
An adult fairy tale with a dark side.

00:17:03.136 --> 00:17:07.658
- I think that the movie
comes from a very very dark,

00:17:07.658 --> 00:17:10.241
very genuine place in my heart.

00:17:11.135 --> 00:17:15.302
And I think that sincerity
always finds an audience,

00:17:17.753 --> 00:17:19.813
no matter how small, no matter how large.

00:17:19.813 --> 00:17:22.401
Is Pan's Labyrinth going
to be, is it going to make

00:17:22.401 --> 00:17:24.455
the same money that Little Nemo did?

00:17:24.455 --> 00:17:27.909
No, but whatever audience it touches,

00:17:27.909 --> 00:17:29.952
it's going to touch really deeply.

00:17:29.952 --> 00:17:33.389
Will Pan find people
that fall in love with it

00:17:33.389 --> 00:17:35.932
rabidly in the world, yes.

00:17:35.932 --> 00:17:37.830
Will it find people that never understand

00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:39.676
what the appeal is, yes.

00:17:39.676 --> 00:17:43.077
But it's a film that comes
from a genuine place.

00:17:43.077 --> 00:17:45.490
It has the two things that
are absolutely necessary

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:47.990
to make film, balls and heart.

00:17:51.332 --> 00:17:52.852
- Thank you so much for watching,

00:17:52.852 --> 00:17:55.506
I made this video
because David DJ Pachenco

00:17:55.506 --> 00:17:57.677
asked me to so if there's
a famous entrepreneur

00:17:57.677 --> 00:17:59.308
that you want me to profile next

00:17:59.308 --> 00:18:00.473
leave it in the comments below

00:18:00.473 --> 00:18:01.617
and I'll see what I can do.

00:18:01.617 --> 00:18:03.729
I also love to know which of
the points you heard today

00:18:03.729 --> 00:18:05.427
had the biggest impact on you.

00:18:05.427 --> 00:18:06.754
What are you going to take from this video

00:18:06.754 --> 00:18:08.838
that you're going to immediately apply

00:18:08.838 --> 00:18:10.830
to your life or to your business some how,

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:12.462
leave it in the comments,
I'm curious to see

00:18:12.462 --> 00:18:13.766
what you guys have to say.

00:18:13.766 --> 00:18:15.684
Finally, I want to give a
quick shout out to Armen.

00:18:15.684 --> 00:18:17.319
Armen thank you so much
for picking up a copy

00:18:17.319 --> 00:18:19.672
of my book, Your One
Word and for reading it

00:18:19.672 --> 00:18:23.230
while having your soup as
well, I appreciate that.

00:18:23.230 --> 00:18:24.063
I hope you're enjoying the book

00:18:24.063 --> 00:18:25.095
and thank you for the support.

00:18:25.095 --> 00:18:26.548
Thank you guys again for watching,

00:18:26.548 --> 00:18:28.371
I believe in you, I hope you continue

00:18:28.371 --> 00:18:32.248
to believe in yourself, aim
whatever your one word is,

00:18:32.248 --> 00:18:34.665
much love, I'll see you soon.

00:18:40.588 --> 00:18:43.409
- I honestly think that a film in complete

00:18:43.409 --> 00:18:46.664
and absolute freedom with unlimited budget

00:18:46.664 --> 00:18:50.447
is a, impossible and b, undesirable.

00:18:50.447 --> 00:18:51.991
I don't think you want that.

00:18:51.991 --> 00:18:56.321
I think you want, I think
film is most definitely

00:18:56.321 --> 00:18:59.466
an exercise between
desire and restriction.

00:18:59.466 --> 00:19:03.633
You need to have, my actor
from Cronos, Frederico Luppi.

00:19:06.053 --> 00:19:07.395
He said to me something beautiful

00:19:07.395 --> 00:19:10.788
because we were doing a scene and he said,

00:19:10.788 --> 00:19:12.350
what do you want me to do here

00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:14.627
and I said whatever you
want, it's written for you,

00:19:14.627 --> 00:19:17.220
and he said no, I can
only be completely free

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:19.539
if you give me some confines.

00:19:19.539 --> 00:19:21.774
He says then within that I'll be free.

00:19:21.774 --> 00:19:23.937
And I understood, that was a beautiful way

00:19:23.937 --> 00:19:27.004
of putting it and I think that's art.

00:19:27.004 --> 00:19:31.809
But certainly filmmaking,
you have, you define those

00:19:31.809 --> 00:19:35.976
sort of goals and posts and
you run around in there.

00:19:37.395 --> 00:19:38.927
- [Interviewer] So do
you have ideas and go oh,

00:19:38.927 --> 00:19:40.024
Guillermo that's too much.

00:19:40.024 --> 00:19:41.481
- Oh yes, all the time.

00:19:41.481 --> 00:19:44.651
All the time because I
think that your ambition

00:19:44.651 --> 00:19:47.728
should always be more than your budget.

00:19:47.728 --> 00:19:50.828
It doesn't matter what your budget is.

00:19:50.828 --> 00:19:53.138
I remember talking to Alfonso Cuaron

00:19:53.138 --> 00:19:54.744
while he was prepping Harry Potter

00:19:54.744 --> 00:19:57.648
and he was saying we're
trying to contain it

00:19:57.648 --> 00:20:01.034
and he gave me the number,
whatever 120 or 220,

00:20:01.034 --> 00:20:02.531
and I went what?

00:20:02.531 --> 00:20:04.360
And it's true, you're trying to contain.

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:06.994
In a movie like that you're
still trying to contain.

00:20:06.994 --> 00:20:10.244
The myth of people that see a big movie

00:20:12.394 --> 00:20:15.085
and say oh, they give me
the money I could do that,

00:20:15.085 --> 00:20:17.113
it's a myth, it's entirely a myth,

00:20:17.113 --> 00:20:20.492
and it comes with some
absolute lack of knowledge

00:20:20.492 --> 00:20:21.656
of how these things operate

00:20:21.656 --> 00:20:23.752
because it's a military operation.

00:20:23.752 --> 00:20:28.529
And the larger it becomes
still the budget is a monster

00:20:28.529 --> 00:20:31.083
and a wall and you're
running a train against it

00:20:31.083 --> 00:20:33.493
and you need to know where to stop.

00:20:33.493 --> 00:20:37.729
Watch as many movies of
as many eras as you can.

00:20:37.729 --> 00:20:40.787
When I was coming up through the ranks,

00:20:40.787 --> 00:20:42.733
there was a thing called a cinema club

00:20:42.733 --> 00:20:46.379
in the '70s where you threaded 16mm film

00:20:46.379 --> 00:20:50.939
and you projected it and I
became familiar with people

00:20:50.939 --> 00:20:54.433
that are important to me like (mumbles),

00:20:54.433 --> 00:20:58.223
like Laurel and Hardy, the Three Stooges,

00:20:58.223 --> 00:21:01.108
you became familiar with Renoir,

00:21:01.108 --> 00:21:02.397
you became familiar with everything

00:21:02.397 --> 00:21:06.480
from comedy to drama you
can see Preston Sturges,

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:11.223
don't just limit yourself
to what is out there,

00:21:11.223 --> 00:21:15.390
or the top 10 at the box
office, that is the main tool,

00:21:16.959 --> 00:21:19.376
and live film history because

00:21:21.412 --> 00:21:22.818
you are part of that narrative.

00:21:22.818 --> 00:21:25.204
Even if it's not film anymore you are

00:21:25.204 --> 00:21:27.280
a part of that narrative tradition.

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:30.647
And the second thing
I feel very important,

00:21:30.647 --> 00:21:35.082
do not get wrapped in the
industry side of this.

00:21:35.082 --> 00:21:36.928
That's what I find really heartbreaking

00:21:36.928 --> 00:21:39.047
that people are now a lot of times

00:21:39.047 --> 00:21:42.273
you're discussing film
with people that love film

00:21:42.273 --> 00:21:45.513
and it's like you're
listening to an agent.

00:21:45.513 --> 00:21:48.056
From an agency or the head of a studio,

00:21:48.056 --> 00:21:51.098
they know the grosses,
they know who's doing this,

00:21:51.098 --> 00:21:54.871
that's absolutely not
important, the important thing

00:21:54.871 --> 00:21:58.171
is that tradition you
are going to belong to

00:21:58.171 --> 00:22:00.612
and do not get caught on the other side,

00:22:00.612 --> 00:22:03.229
stay true to the other side that needs you

00:22:03.229 --> 00:22:05.159
and needs to exist and urgently.

